A||||3||Jncomutt||Oh No... What have I done??  Belt Broke!||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||12-07-2001||08:33 PM||||I was drivin my 84 down a narrow 2 lane road with lots of traffic when I started to smell this burning.  I smelt it a little more, but just thought it happened to be the fact there was one of those big Ford F450 (or whatever) diesel trucks in frnt of me.  All off a sudden, as I applied the gas, I heard this horrid rapting noise from the rear, A light pops on and I look down to notice my temp gauge soaring and the light glowing.  I want to pull over, but due to the narrowness, there is just barely enough room for the cars to go in each direction so I shut the car off, and allow it to coast to where there was a little snack shop.  Well I pop open the trunk and notice the alt/wp belt hanging by the AC belt.  Smoke was coming up from valve cover all over.  I opened the oil add cap and smoke came streaming out as well.  I look down at the newly installed exhaust tips, and see the same thing, along with a puddle of antifreeze forming from the leaking overflow bottle upfront.  I let the car cool down as I managed to reattach the belt using the .5" open end I carry and the lug wrench to pry.  I started the car up and everything checked in fine.  The alt gauge was reading ok as was the temp.  I get back on the road and keep driving.  I then hear this rappin, tappin noise so I pull over once more.  Temp gauge a lil high, but no light on.  I look at the belt to notice it is in 3 pieces.  held together by a thin string it appears.  <P>Well after a 7.5 mile jog down route 66 to advance auto.  I manage the 7.5 miles back where I put on the new belt.  I drove home and the car was cool, never got warm.  I also didn't hear any noises, and the car ran as smooth as always.  I seems to be fine now, I just want to check with you guys if they're something else I should be concerned with before this becomes my daily driver again.  Thanks in advance, and CHECK YOUR BELTS!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||banditbalz||12-07-2001||08:47 PM||||Had the exact same thing happen to a '84 duke that I drove in college. They don't call them "Iron Dukes" for nothing!. Those heads can take a lot of heat. The tapping noise you heard is of some concern though. You mentioned it went away after replacing  the belt, so it may have just been the rocker arms (hot oil has a lower viscosity). Everything should be fine, but would change the oil and keep an eye on the coolant level. If you find you are constantly adding coolant, you may have cracked the head, but i doubt it. Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||11
Z||000002||Jake_Dragon||12-07-2001||09:00 PM||||Or the belt slappin around.<BR>I did the same thing in my coupe  <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/dung.gif">  put a new belt on and it is fine.||||reg||1
